course: everyday
lesson: 465
title: The new kid and the first day
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Ada starts at Sam's school in March, halfway through the year. Everyone already has a group. At break she stands by the wall pretending to read the fire notice for the fifth time. Sam remembers being new in Year 1, the way the playground looked as big as the sea. He walks over: «Want to play tig? We need one more.» Ada's so relieved she can't even speak; she just nods and runs. One easy invite can change somebody's whole year.
explain: Being new is lonely, and nobody has to be mean for it to hurt. Connor's first day at a new school, nobody saves him a seat at lunch, so he eats his sandwiches in the toilets. The next day a dinner lady spots him alone and walks him to a table with a nice group, who shove up and make room. Mia's teacher gives her a buddy, Hannah, who shows her the toilets, the canteen and where not to sit («that's the Year 6 table, they're well territorial»). By Friday they sit together by choice. When Kuba arrives from Poland with only a bit of English, Lily's class makes him a card that says «cześć» — hello in Polish — and he teaches them a tongue twister nobody can say.
explain: New places have new words, and they can sound rude when they're friendly. In Liverpool a boy says to Jake, «Alright, la? You sound posh.» «La» is Scouse for lad. Jake laughs, «I'm not posh, I'm from Stoke,» and they're mates. Chloe, new from Belfast, gets asked «why d'you talk funny?» «Cos I'm Irish, so I am,» she says, and within a week she's teaching them «what's the craic?» — Irish for «what's going on?». In Perth, Maddie gets called «Pom», Australian for an English person. «Yeah nah, I'm a Geordie actually,» she says, and explains.
explain: Sometimes you have to go first. At a new swimming club Sam stands at the side until he asks a boy, «You doing the fifty metres?» They talk times, then Minecraft. Jake's teacher sits him next to quiet Zack; three days of silence, then Zack picks up Jake's dropped pencil case. «Ta.» Silence. On Friday Zack shows him a dragon drawing; Jake says it's sick; by half-term they're mates. When Kieran tells a new boy «you can't play, we've got enough», a teacher has a quiet word — and the new boy turns out better at football than all of them. Lily's mum, who moved country at nine, says, «The first day was the worst of my life. By Christmas I had three best friends.»
fact: Being new is lonely even when nobody is unkind; often nobody has noticed.
fact: A simple invitation to someone standing alone can change their whole year.
fact: If you are new and have nobody to sit with, tell a teacher or dinner lady; they can help you find a group.
fact: Being a buddy to a new pupil is an important job to take seriously.
fact: Local slang can sound rude to a newcomer when it is meant friendly; ask or laugh along before deciding someone is an enemy.
fact: Learning a word or two of a new pupil's language, like «cześć» (hello) and «dziękuję» (thank you) in Polish, makes them feel welcome.
fact: Turning new people away from a game is unkind and can mean missing out on a great friend.
fact: Answering curious questions about where you are from with a smile can make your difference a good thing.
fact: Nicknames for newcomers are often teasing rather than hate, but if one is nasty, tell a teacher.
fact: Some friendships start slowly; don't give up in the first week.
fact: Most new pupils settle in and make friends within a few months.
fact: In Liverpool «la» means lad; in Ireland «craic» means fun or news; in Australia «Pom» means an English person; «ta» is British for thanks.
exercise: Why was Ada pretending to read the fire notice? | She was new and had nobody to play with | She loves fire safety rules more than anything | The teacher told her to | She was on fire duty
exercise: What did Sam say to Ada? | «Want to play tig? We need one more.» | «Why are you on your own?» | «That's my wall, move.» | «Are you lost or what?»
exercise: Why did Sam know how Ada felt? | He'd been new himself once | Ada told him everything | He read about it in a book | The teacher explained it
exercise: Connor ate in the toilets on his first day. What should a new kid in that spot do? | Tell a teacher or dinner lady | Keep eating in the toilets | Go home at lunch | Sit at the Year 6 table
exercise: What did the dinner lady do for Connor? | Walked him to a table with a nice group | Told him off for hiding in the toilets at lunch | Gave him extra pudding | Phoned his mum to collect him
exercise: What does a school buddy do for a new kid? | Shows them round and helps them settle | Does their homework for them in the first week | Tells them where to hide | Chooses their friends for them
exercise: «Alright, la?» What does «la» mean in Liverpool? | Lad | Leave | Lunch | Loser
exercise: Was the Liverpool boy being mean to Jake? | No, it was friendly in his own way | Yes, he wanted a fight | Yes, he hates people from Stoke | No, he was talking to someone else
exercise: Chloe teaches them «what's the craic?» What does it mean? | What's going on? | Who broke it this time? | What's for lunch? | Where's the toilet?
exercise: What did Lily's class do to welcome Kuba from Poland? | Made a card with «cześć» (hello) on it | Made him speak only English all day, every day | Left him to work it out | Gave him a new name
exercise: What did Kieran's «we've got enough» cost him? | A new boy who was the best player | A detention every day for a whole month | His place in the team | His football
exercise: In Perth the kids call Maddie a «Pom». What does it mean? | An English person | A small fluffy dog | A new girl | A cheerleader
exercise: How did Sam make a friend at the new swimming club? | Asked a boy about the fifty metres | Waited until someone spoke to him | Splashed everyone to get attention | Swam fastest so they'd notice
exercise: How long did it take Jake and Zack to become mates? | Until half-term, starting very slowly | One minute | They never did | Ten years
exercise: What did Lily's mum say about her own first day? | It was awful, but by Christmas she had three best friends | It was the best day ever and she made ten friends by lunch | She never made a friend | She went back home the next day
exercise: «Ta,» says Jake. What does «ta» mean? | Thanks | Hello | Goodbye | Sorry
test: «new girl's just standing there like a lemon, weird». What's most likely going on? | She's new and nobody's asked her to play | She's ignoring everyone on purpose because she's posh | She loves standing still | She's waiting for a bus
test: Kieran: «we've got enough players, new kid can watch». What would most likely happen if he let him play? | He might find a great new teammate | The game would be ruined | Everyone would quit | The new kid would take over the school
test: «they called me Pom, that's well racist». What's more likely? | It's mostly cheeky teasing; tell a teacher if it gets nasty | It's always the worst insult there is, so hit back at once | It means she's pretty | It's a type of pudding
test: In a cartoon, the new kid becomes the most popular in school by lunchtime on day one. What's more usual? | It takes a few weeks or months to settle | Everyone always loves the new kid at once | New kids never make friends | Only new kids with dogs make friends
test: You're the buddy for a new girl but your mates want you at football. What's right? | Do the buddy job properly first | Ditch her, because football's more fun | Tell her to follow you around silently | Give her to someone else secretly
test: Jake and Zack haven't spoken for three days. Jake: «he's dead boring, I'm asking to move». Better idea? | Give it time, slow friendships can be great | Move on day three | Tell the teacher Zack is boring | Ignore him forever
test: «why d'you talk funny?» How did Chloe turn that question into a good thing? | She answered with a smile and taught them Irish words | She said they talked funny instead and walked off laughing | She cried and ran off | She stopped talking
test: You moved school and you're eating lunch alone for the third day. What's the best step? | Tell a teacher or dinner lady | Keep it secret, it'll pass | Stop eating lunch | Pretend you're on your phone forever
test: Kuba speaks only a little English. Which would help him most? | Learning to say hello in Polish | Talking very loudly and slowly at him | Leaving him to work it all out alone | Correcting every mistake he makes
test: What would most likely have happened to Ada if Sam hadn't asked her to play? | She'd have stood alone longer | She'd have taken over the game of tig | She'd have moved school that day | Nothing, she wanted to be alone
test: Lily's nervous about starting a new club. What does her mum's story suggest? | The first day is hard, but it gets better | Never try new places | First days are always fun | Only grown-ups find it hard
exam: Identify the action most likely to relieve the isolation of a pupil who joins a school midway through the year. | A simple invitation to participate in play | A public enquiry as to why she is alone | The recommendation that she wait for others | The suggestion that she read the notices
exam: Given that a new pupil has no companion at lunch, ascertain the most appropriate course of action for that pupil. | To inform a teacher or a member of the lunchtime staff | To eat alone in the toilets indefinitely | To return home without permission | To sit at the table reserved for older pupils
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises regional expressions addressed to a newcomer, such as «Alright, la?»? | They are frequently friendly though they may sound otherwise | They are invariably intended as insults | They should be reported to the head teacher at once | They indicate that a fight is imminent
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the period required for a new pupil to form friendships. | It frequently extends over several weeks or months | It is invariably completed on the first day | Friendships cannot be formed after the first week | It depends solely upon the pupil's possessions
